        "original_id": "86be6a00-23a3-5eeb-9bff-630de771bd48",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: ʻUmi-a-Līloa, Liloa, Charles Kanaʻina, Hakau, Piʻilani\n- Roles: aliʻi-ai-moku, High Chief\n- Places: Hawaiʻi, Hawaiʻi Island, Maui\n- Concepts: Hawaiian religion, Native Hawaiians, polytheism, animism, deities, spirits, religious authority\n\nFacts:\n- ʻUmi-a-Līloa was a fifteenth-century ruling aliʻi-ai-moku, or district high chief, of Hawaiʻi.\n- ʻUmi-a-Līloa inherited the religious authority of Hawaiʻi from his father, High Chief Liloa.\n- Liloa's line was traced unbroken to Hawaiian creation.\n- Hawaiian religion encompasses the indigenous religious beliefs and practices of the Native Hawaiians.\n- Hawaiian religion is polytheistic and animistic.\n- Hawaiian religion includes belief in many deities and spirits.\n- Hawaiian religion teaches that spirits are found in non-human beings and objects such as animals, the waves, and the sky.\n- Charles Kanaʻina was a descendant of figures from ancient Hawaiian history, including Liloa, Hakau, ʻUmi-a-Līloa of Hawaiʻi Island, and Piʻilani of Maui.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4fd6b682-c29a-52d7-a321-4a1a8811188c",
        "text": "Page: Umi-a-Liloa. Sentence: ʻUmi-a-Līloa (fifteenth century) was a ruling ali'i-ai-moku (district high chief of Hawai'i) who inherited religious authority of Hawai'i from his father, High Chief Liloa, whose line is traced, unbroken to Hawaiian \"creation\".\nPage: Hawaiian religion. Sentence: Hawaiian religion encompasses the indigenous religious beliefs and practices of the Native Hawaiians.\nPage: Hawaiian religion. Sentence: It is polytheistic and animistic, with a belief in many deities and spirits, including the belief that spirits are found in non-human beings and objects such as animals, the waves, and the sky.\nPage: Charles Kanaʻina. Sentence: Kanaʻina was a descendant of several figures from ancient Hawaiian history, including Liloa, Hakau and Umi-a-Liloa of Hawaiʻ i Island as well as Piilani of Maui.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
